#!/usr/bin/env python3
# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""
isemon.py - a banning daemon for Cisco ISE.

Two Identity Services Engine nodes in an HA pair write their logs here over
syslog. One of them is doing the authenticating at any moment and the other is
quiet, and which one that is changes without warning - so both are read, all
the time, and what comes out of them is put into one timeline.

Out of the failed attempts it reads who is trying to get into the VPN and
cannot. An address that is already on one of the public blocklists is kept out
for good the first time it appears. An address that is not on any list is
given the ladder: so many tries in so many minutes earns ten minutes, then an
hour, then a day, then for good.

What is kept out here is carried to a Mikrotik router as an address list, so
the packets stop before they arrive. The same router can be given a second
list holding whole countries, refreshed from the country database.

One file. The Python standard library and nothing else: AlmaLinux 9 has
everything this needs in the base system.

It runs as a systemd service and answers a localhost HTTP API that the PHP
dashboard reads. Every part of it is also a command:

    isemon --status              what it is doing, in one screen
    isemon --failed              the failed attempts, newest first
    isemon --passed              the successful logins
    isemon --blocked             who is kept out, and until when
    isemon --ban ADDR            keep an address out by hand
    isemon --unban ADDR          let one back in
    isemon --whitelist           the addresses nothing may ever ban
    isemon --allow ADDR          add one to that list
    isemon --users               the people who may open the dashboard
    isemon --add-user NAME       make one (--role fulladmin|banadmin|readonly)
    isemon --passwd NAME         set or reset somebody's password
    isemon --mikrotik            what the router is carrying
    isemon --geo-sync            fill the country list on the router
    isemon --settings            every setting, as it stands
    isemon --backup              a copy of the database, now
    isemon --dump                a dump of it in the engine's own format, now
    isemon --check               a health check against the running daemon
    isemon                       run the daemon in the foreground
"""

# Bumped by hand with every change to this file.
__version__ = '1.10.253'
# How far back it is safe to go from this version (1.10.232): the oldest one
# that can still run on a database this version has used - the same tables,
# the same columns, nothing written that it would misread. Going back to it
# leaves the database exactly as it is: nothing is copied or put back, which
# on a database of a terabyte is the only way going back can be done at all.
# Raised whenever a version changes what older ones read; test/fixes232.py
# holds the shape of the database it was set for and fails when that shape
# changes, so that it is never forgotten. Root reads it from its own copy of
# the program (isemon_upgrade.py), never from what was asked.
DOWNGRADE_FLOOR = '1.10.226'

